# Name: Amstrad CPC
#
# Amstrad CPC (464/664/6128 etc.) firmware character set, bytes
# 0x80-0xFF - block/box-drawing graphics, accented letters, Greek
# letters, card suits, and a handful of pictograms. 0x00-0x7F is plain
# ASCII (unlisted below, kept as identity); entirely above the 7-bit
# ASCII range, so unlike CP437/ATASCII this whole block carries none of
# their VT100-control-code conflict risk (see TODO.md Abschnitt 6.3).
# 0x80-0x9F doubles as the ISO/ECMA-48 "C1" 8-bit control range in some
# other 8-bit-terminal contexts, same as the Acorn/RISC OS charset in
# this same folder - KomportEmulation does not implement any C1 control
# function today regardless of charset, so this does not newly break a
# working control function, only makes some previously-invisible bytes
# visible as CPC characters instead.
#
# Source: Wikipedia "Amstrad CPC character set" (raw table).
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amstrad_CPC_character_set
#
# 27 bytes - all of 0xC0-0xCA, 0xCE, all of 0xD8-0xDF, 0xEF, and
# 0xF8-0xFD - map to U+FFFD (REPLACEMENT CHARACTER) rather than a real
# glyph: the source's own table assigns them real, distinct glyphs, but
# every one of those specific code points lies in Unicode's "Symbols
# for Legacy Computing" block (added in Unicode 13.0-16.0) and is above
# 0xFFFF - this file format has no astral/surrogate-pair support at all
# (see komportcharset.cpp's loadCustomCharsetFile(), "no surrogate-pair/
# astral support"), so none of them can be represented directly. (Three
# of these - 0xEF, 0xFC, 0xFD - had no Unicode assignment whatsoever
# before Unicode 16.0, per the source article; the rest are simply out
# of this format's representable range for an unrelated reason.)
# All 27 are mapped to U+FFFD instead of left unlisted, on purpose: an
# unlisted byte falls back to plain identity, which would display as a
# plausible-looking but simply wrong Latin-1/control-picture character
# (Codex review finding) rather than visibly signal "a real character
# exists here that this file cannot show". Expect the app's own
# duplicate-mapping warning for these 27 bytes when this file loads -
# harmless and expected, since they intentionally share one target
# code point.
